clusters.verdict_basis:
Several outlets report that Vance spoke at a Ken Paxton event in Tyler, Texas, on Tuesday, Oct. 13, 2026. A crowd member shouted that they loved him. He replied, "In a totally platonic way, to be clear. I'm the vice president, not James Talarico" (
Raw Story
). Critics, including MS NOW's Zeeshan Aleem and commentator Adam Cochran, called it a homophobic jab (
Raw Story
). HuffPost (via Yahoo) and the Los Angeles Blade also covered the backlash (
HuffPost
; 
LA Blade
). The claim's wording, "seemingly homophobic," is hedged and matches the reporting. The backlash is documented. Whether the joke is homophobic is a characterization the critics made. Most of the coverage comes from left-leaning or opinion-oriented outlets. I found no direct response from Vance or the White House. The LA Blade said it had not heard back from the White House (
LA Blade
).

Reporting and records support the claim. Pew's validated-voter study of 2024 found Trump won 48% of Latino voters, up from 2020, and that his gains also reached Black, Asian and young voters (
Washington Post
; 
Axios
). In Texas, 
the Texas Tribune/ProPublica reported that Trump won 14 of 18 border counties and all four Rio Grande Valley counties. That included Starr County, which went Republican for the first time since 1896.
 CNN's analysis described 2024 as one of historic gains for Trump with Latinos, and the Tribune notes the 2026 debate over whether these gains will last (
CNN
). The claim is worded generally and makes no number or date assertion that could be wrong. Pew does caution that much of the shift came from differential turnout rather than voters switching parties, but that does not contradict "inroads."
